== Production ==
=== Development ===
In May 2018, [[The CW]] president [[Mark Pedowitz]] and ''[[Arrow (TV series)|Arrow]]'' lead [[Stephen Amell]] announced at The CW's [[Upfront (advertising)|upfront]] presentation that [[Batwoman]] would be introduced in the [[Arrowverse]] series' 2018 crossover, "[[Elseworlds (Arrowverse)|Elseworlds]]", which aired in December 2018, fighting alongside the other Arrowverse heroes, with [[Gotham City]] also appearing.<ref name="BatwomanCrossover" /><ref name="ElseworldsTitle" /> In July 2018, it was reported that The CW was planning to develop a series around the character, to air in 2019 if picked up. The series, said to only be a "script-development deal", was written by [[Caroline Dries]], who would also serve as an executive producer with [[Greg Berlanti]], Sarah Schecter, and the character's co-creator [[Geoff Johns]]. The series would be produced by [[Berlanti Productions]] and [[Mad Ghost Productions]] in association with [[Warner Bros. Television]].<ref name="BatwomanSeries" /> The following month, Pedowitz noted the pilot would be completed "for mid-season".<ref name="PedowitzAug2018" />

In December 2018, Dries submitted a "strong" script for a potential pilot episode, according to Nellie Andreeva of ''[[Deadline Hollywood]]''.<ref name="PilotOrderDeadline" /> That led to the series receiving a pilot order from The CW the next month, to be considered for a series order in the [[2019–20 United States network television schedule|2019–20 television season]].<ref name="PilotOrderTHR" /><ref name="PilotOrderDeadline" /><ref name="PilotOrderEW" /> As of April 2019, the series was considered "a lock" at The CW, and reportedly had a writing staff in place.<ref name="PilotBuzz" /> On May 7, 2019, The CW ordered the show to series.<ref name="SeriesOrder" /> On October 25, 2019, the series was picked up for a full season of 22 episodes.<ref name="Fullseason22" /> On January 7, 2020, the series was renewed for a [[Batwoman (season 2)|second season]], which premiered on January 17, 2021.<ref name="S2Renewal" /><ref name="2021SeasonStartDates">{{Cite web |last=Ausiello |first=Michael |date=October 29, 2020 |title=The CW Sets Delayed Premiere Dates for Flash, Superman & Lois, Riverdale, Charmed, Batwoman and More |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-cw-premiere-dates-2021-the-flash-superman-and-lois-1234589328/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201029164556/https://tvline.com/2020/10/29/the-cw-premiere-dates-2021-the-flash-superman-and-lois/ |archive-date=October 29, 2020 |access-date=October 29, 2020 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

On February 3, 2021, The CW renewed the series for a third season which premiered on October 13, 2021,<ref name="S3Renewal">{{Cite web |last=White |first=Peter |date=February 3, 2021 |title=The CW Renews 12 Series Including Freshman Drama 'Walker' |url=https://deadline.com/2021/02/the-cw-major-renewal-move-new-seasons-12-shows-walker-1234686788/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210203211446/https://deadline.com/2021/02/the-cw-major-renewal-move-new-seasons-12-shows-walker-1234686788/ |archive-date=February 3, 2021 |access-date=February 3, 2021 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref><ref name="S3Premiere">{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=June 15, 2021 |title=The CW Sets Fall Premiere Dates for ''The Flash'', ''Walker'', ''4400'' and 11 Others |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-cw-fall-premiere-dates-the-flash-walker-riverdale-1234683482/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210616040119/https://tvline.com/2021/06/15/the-cw-fall-premiere-dates-the-flash-walker-riverdale/ |archive-date=June 16, 2021 |access-date=June 15, 2021 |website=TVLine}}</ref> and the finale aired on March 2, 2022.<ref name="TFCMarch022022">{{Cite web |title=LISTINGS, wednesday, march 02, 2022 |url=http://www.thefutoncritic.com/listings/2022/03/02/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20220731043948/http://www.thefutoncritic.com/listings/2022/03/02/ |archive-date=July 31, 2022 |access-date=January 22, 2022 |website=[[The Futon Critic]]}}</ref> On April 29, 2022, The CW canceled the series after three seasons.<ref name="Cancellation" />

=== Casting ===
Casting for Kate Kane was expected to begin after the announcement of the intended series in May 2018,<ref name="BatwomanSeriesDeadline" /> with the intention of casting an openly gay actress.<ref name="BatwomanCasting" /> In August, [[Ruby Rose]] was cast as [[Kate Kane (Arrowverse)|Kate Kane / Batwoman]].<ref name="RoseCast" /> In late January 2019, [[Meagan Tandy]], [[Camrus Johnson]] and Nicole Kang were cast in the series regular roles as [[Sophie Moore (Arrowverse)|Sophie Moore]], [[Luke Fox (Arrowverse)|Luke Fox]] and [[Mary Hamilton (Arrowverse)|Mary Hamilton]], respectively.<ref name="TandyJohnsonKang" /> This was followed shortly by the casting of [[Rachel Skarsten]] as [[Alice (Arrowverse)|Alice]],<ref name="Skarsten" /> [[Dougray Scott]] as [[Jacob Kane (Arrowverse)|Jacob Kane]],<ref name="ScottCastSiegaDirector" /> and [[Elizabeth Anweis]] as [[Catherine Hamilton-Kane (Arrowverse)|Catherine Hamilton-Kane]].<ref name="Anweis" />

The casting of Rose as Batwoman was met by backlash on social media and received intense criticism.<ref name="Cavna" /><ref name="Price">{{Cite web |last=Price, Emily |date=August 13, 2018 |title=Ruby Rose Quits Twitter Over 'Batwoman' Casting Backlash |url=http://fortune.com/2018/08/13/ruby-rose-quits-twitter-batwoman-casting-backlash/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190524211521/http://fortune.com/2018/08/13/ruby-rose-quits-twitter-batwoman-casting-backlash/ |archive-date=May 24, 2019 |access-date=May 16, 2019 |website=[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]]}}</ref> DC Comics, which owns the rights to the long-time comic book superheroine Batwoman, reintroduced the character in 2006 as a gay Jewish woman. Some online reactions attacked Rose for not being Jewish, while the main focus of the criticism was the assertion that the fact that she considers herself [[gender fluidity|gender-fluid]] made her "not gay enough."<ref name="Price" /> Rose left [[Twitter]] and deactivated public commenting on her [[Instagram]] account following the backlash.<ref name="Cavna">{{Cite news |last=Cavna |first=Michael |date=August 13, 2018 |title=Ruby Rose Leaves Twitter After Criticism That She Isn't 'Gay Enough' for Batwoman |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/comic-riffs/wp/2018/08/13/ruby-rose-leaves-twitter-after-criticism-of-her-batwoman-casting/?noredirect=on |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190725141026/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/comic-riffs/wp/2018/08/13/ruby-rose-leaves-twitter-after-criticism-of-her-batwoman-casting/?noredirect=on |archive-date=July 25, 2019 |access-date=May 16, 2019 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref>

In July 2021, [[Robin Givens]], [[Victoria Cartagena]] and Nick Creegan were cast as new series regulars for the third season.<ref name="Givens">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=July 6, 2021 |title='Batwoman': Robin Givens Joins Season 3 Of CW Drama As Series Regular |url=https://deadline.com/2021/07/batwoman-robin-givens-season-3-cast-cw-series-regular-1234786886/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210706184611/https://deadline.com/2021/07/batwoman-robin-givens-season-3-cast-cw-series-regular-1234786886/ |archive-date=July 6, 2021 |access-date=July 6, 2021 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref><ref name="Cartagena">{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=July 20, 2021 |title='Batwoman': Victoria Cartagena To Play Renee Montoya In Season 3 Of CW Drama |url=https://deadline.com/2021/07/batwoman-victoria-cartagena-renee-montoya-dc-hero-season-3-cw-series-1234796596/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210720172155/https://deadline.com/2021/07/batwoman-victoria-cartagena-renee-montoya-dc-hero-season-3-cw-series-1234796596/ |archive-date=July 20, 2021 |access-date=July 20, 2021 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref><ref name="Creegan">{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=July 26, 2021 |title='Batwoman': Nick Creegan Joins Season 3 As New Series Regular |url=https://deadline.com/2021/07/batwoman-nick-creegan-season-3-series-regular-marquis-jet-1234800087/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210726190136/https://deadline.com/2021/07/batwoman-nick-creegan-season-3-series-regular-marquis-jet-1234800087/ |archive-date=July 26, 2021 |access-date=July 26, 2021 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref> In August 2021, [[Bridget Regan]] was cast in the recurring role of [[List of Batwoman characters#Poison Ivy|Poison Ivy]] for the third season.<ref name="Regan">{{Cite web |last=Del Rosario |first=Alexandra |date=August 30, 2021 |title='Batwoman': Bridget Regan To Play Poison Ivy In Season 3 Of CW Drama |url=https://deadline.com/2021/08/batwoman-bridget-regan-play-poison-ivy-in-season-3-cw-1234824460/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210830215154/https://deadline.com/2021/08/batwoman-bridget-regan-play-poison-ivy-in-season-3-cw-1234824460/ |archive-date=August 30, 2021 |access-date=August 30, 2021 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref>

